/* book: lead: An autopilot is the control part of GNC for an aircraft: a set of feedback loops that move the elevator, ailerons and rudder through servos to hold what the pilot or the FMS selects, such as a pitch attitude, an altitude, a bank angle or a heading. Fast inner loops hold attitude; slower outer loops hold the flight path by giving the inner loops their commands. Some syllabi call the lateral half th */
/* book Figure 6.3.1: Each axis is two loops, one inside the other. The solid inner loop holds an attitude (pitch or bank) with angle and rate feedback; the dashed outer loop holds a path quantity (altitude or heading) by changing the inner loop’s command. Notice that the outer loops never drive a servo directly. */
